WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:57.910
It’s the

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:59.660
biggest scandal in US history.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:05.679
On August 8, 1974, Richard Milhouse Nixon
became the first-ever president to resign,

00:01:05.679 --> 00:01:07.779
mere days before Congress could impeach him.

00:01:07.779 --> 00:01:12.929
The cause of this epoch-shaking event was
the Watergate Scandal, an attempted wiretapping

00:01:12.929 --> 00:01:17.399
of the Democratic National Election Headquarters
and its subsequent coverup.

00:01:17.399 --> 00:01:22.350
Even now, after four decades of scandals as
varied as Iran-Contra, Monica Lewinsky, and

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:27.619
whatever the hell was happening in 2016, Watergate
remains the gold-standard of high crimes and

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:28.619
misdemeanours.

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:32.569
“-Gate” has even entered the lexicon as
a suffix denoting scandal.

00:01:32.569 --> 00:01:37.369
But while most of us are aware of Watergate,
how much do we know about the man behind it

00:01:37.369 --> 00:01:38.369
all?

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:42.670
Born into a blue collar family in 1913, Richard
Nixon was a natural political animal.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:46.689
Throughout his House and Senate careers, he
earned a reputation as a formidable opponent.

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:50.729
First as Eisenhower’s VP, then as President
in his own right, he oversaw initiatives that

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:51.899
transformed America.

00:01:51.899 --> 00:01:58.140
Yet the 37th President would wind up dynamiting
his own legacy in the most-dramatic way possible.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:05.109
Flawed, paranoid, brilliant; this is the tale
of Richard Nixon, the most-controversial president.

00:02:05.109 --> 00:02:10.420
The Glory of Poverty

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:15.030
As he would never tire of saying, the 37th
President came from a hard background.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:21.810
Born on January 9, 1913, in Yorba Linda, California,
Richard Milhouse Nixon grew up not in a fancy-ass

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:25.230
mansion, but a modest home he shared with
four siblings.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.770
His perpetually-angry father, Frank, owned
a service station and a small, failing citrus

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:33.290
farm that never brought in much more than
a handful of pennies.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:34.670
As Nixon would later reminisce:

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.939
"We were poor, but the glory of it was we
didn't know it."

00:02:37.939 --> 00:02:40.879
However, you could argue this statement was
not entirely accurate.

00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:45.739
For his entire adult life, Nixon would privately
rail against those born with a silver spoon

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:51.140
in their mouths: the Kennedys, or the wealthy
New York Jews he perceived as enemies.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.270
Young Nixon may well have been poor.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:55.750
But he very much seemed to know it.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:00.020
Still, this wasn’t the sort of grinding,
soul-sapping poverty that hollows you out.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:04.499
Growing up, Nixon found time to read stacks
of books, run for class president, and engage

00:03:04.499 --> 00:03:06.920
his Quaker mother in energetic debates.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.970
It was in these good-natured arguments that
he got his first taste of politics.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:14.650
The dawning of the 1920s brought a string
of bad news for the Nixon family.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:18.420
First, the family citrus farm went under,
forcing them to leave their home and move

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.599
to nearby Whittier.

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:24.110
Then, Nixon’s youngest brother Arthur died
in 1925, aged only 7.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:28.500
A few years later, his oldest brother, Harold,
also passed away; suffering a long, lingering

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.700
death from tuberculosis.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.780
It was a traumatic time for the family.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.079
But you wouldn’t know it from looking at
Nixon.

00:03:35.079 --> 00:03:39.719
At Whittier High School, the boy was excelling,
eventually graduating second in his class.

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:43.900
He was offered a scholarship to Harvard but,
alas, the family was too poor to send him

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:44.900
away.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.319
Instead, Nixon had to make do with a local
Quaker college.

00:03:47.319 --> 00:03:51.780
Still, the boy applied himself, earning a
reputation as a debate champion; a great football

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.840
player; and a charismatic actor in the school’s
drama productions.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:04:00.670
When he graduated in 1934, Nixon was so red
hot that Duke University’s law school practically

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.610
threw a scholarship at him.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:06.810
By 1937, Nixon was a 24-year old qualified
lawyer with some of the best grades in the

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:08.110
whole damn state.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:10.150
He should’ve been set for life.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:14.010
But things weren’t so easy for a boy from
a hardscrabble background.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:17.239
Every prestigious law firm Nixon applied to
turned him down.

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:19.169
Stung, Nixon slunk back to Whittier.

00:04:19.169 --> 00:04:24.389
He moved in above his parents’ garage, got
a deadend job at a small law firm and contemplated

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:25.580
how unfair life was.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:29.300
Here he was, a gifted boy with the grades
to match, forced to slum it because… why?

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.870
Because his parents didn’t have enough money?

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:36.740
But while returning to Whittier would leave
a bitter taste Nixon would never shake, it

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.199
did at least have one positive outcome.

00:04:39.199 --> 00:04:41.320
In 1938, Nixon joined a local community theatre.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:46.250
There, at a rehearsal on January 18, he met
Thelma Catherine Ryan.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:49.669
Known to her friends as “Pat”, she was
glamorous, beguiling.

00:04:49.669 --> 00:04:51.810
After driving her home that evening, the young
lawyer declared:

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.530
“I’d like to have a date with you.”

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:54.810
To which she replied:

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.150
“I’m busy.”

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:59.460
Not exactly a fairytale romance.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:00.980
But Nixon persevered.

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:04.560
When he heard Pat enjoyed ice skating - a
sport he’d never tried - he spent weeks

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.629
forcing himself to learn how to skate, just
so he could “accidentally” meet her at

00:05:08.629 --> 00:05:09.629
the rink.

00:05:09.629 --> 00:05:12.220
Finally, after a year as just friends, Pat
caved.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:13.430
She agreed to that date.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:16.970
On June 21, 1940, Nixon and Pat were married.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:20.780
Together, they agreed to keep chasing their
dreams, to keep being ambitious.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:24.620
Two years later, they moved from California
to Washington, D.C, where Nixon got a job

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:26.520
in the Office of Price Administration.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.630
It was 29-year old’s first foray into the
world of politics.

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:35.129
It certainly wouldn’t be his last.

00:05:35.129 --> 00:05:38.759
Street Fighting Man

00:05:38.759 --> 00:05:42.229
In the end, Nixon lasted less than a year
in the Office of Price Administration.

00:05:42.229 --> 00:05:46.860
An FDR New Deal program, it was the embodiment
of big government, something that chilled

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:48.480
Nixon to his soul.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:49.810
He quit in disgust.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:53.160
But rather than return to practicing law,
he joined the Navy.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:54.979
This was a pretty impressive thing to do.

00:05:54.979 --> 00:05:59.319
As a Quaker, Nixon was exempt from serving
- something of a get out of jail free card

00:05:59.319 --> 00:06:02.360
considering the massive bloody war going on.

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:05.569
But Nixon enlisted regardless, serving in
the Pacific.

00:06:05.569 --> 00:06:09.730
Although he never saw combat, he still managed
to pick up two service stars before resigning

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:12.320
his commission in January, 1946.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:16.550
Barely had Nixon swapped his starched, white
uniform for a dull gray suit than a group

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:20.639
of Republicans from his hometown approached
him with an interesting proposition.

00:06:20.639 --> 00:06:25.729
Five term Democrat Jerry Voorhis was up for
reelection in California’s 12th District.

00:06:25.729 --> 00:06:30.139
Despite being soft on Communism, no Republican
had ever been able to unseat him.

00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:34.460
But with a war hero as their candidate, then
maybe… just maybe…

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:38.950
Throughout 1946, Nixon went for the election
with the same bloodymindedness he’d shown

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:40.270
pursuing Pat.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:43.990
Despite his reputation, Voorhis’s actual
connections to Communism were non-existent.

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:47.160
So, Nixon just made them up.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:51.830
At a town hall debate, Nixon flourished a
scrap of paper supposedly linking Voorhis

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.729
to a Communist-linked PAC.

00:06:53.729 --> 00:06:57.210
Nevermind that Nixon was actually talking
about a completely different PAC.

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:00.069
Nevermind that Voorhis was against Communism.

00:07:00.069 --> 00:07:03.370
In voters’ minds, the Democrat was now a
Red.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:06.120
Come November, they voted for Nixon in droves.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:10.620
It was Nixon’s first experience with negative
campaigning - the art of not making yourself

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:14.220
look good, but making your opponent look worse.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:17.199
He was an goddamn genius at it.

00:07:17.199 --> 00:07:21.600
After a short stint in the House - which included
the successful prosecution of Alger Hiss,

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:25.460
a State Department employee turned Soviet
stooge - Nixon ran for the Senate.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:30.060
This time, his campaigning was so negative
it approached absolute zero.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:34.069
Nixon slandered his opponent as "pink right
down to her underwear".

00:07:34.069 --> 00:07:39.449
His staff handed out “pink sheets,” falsely
comparing her voting record to that of Communists.

00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:42.159
As in 1946, it worked.

00:07:42.159 --> 00:07:43.210
Nixon became Senator.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:46.740
However, he also picked up something else
alongside his Senate seat.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:51.620
Aghast at his negative campaign, the California
press branded Nixon “Tricky Dick.”

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:54.639
It was a nickname that would stick with him
for life.

00:07:54.639 --> 00:07:56.740
But what did Dick Nixon care?

00:07:56.740 --> 00:08:01.610
From a bright, blue collar kid spurned by
elites, he had gone on to become a Washington

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:02.610
insider.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:05.330
And his meteoric rise wouldn’t stop there.

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:09.879
In 1952, Republican nominee Dwight D. Eisenhower
picked Tricky Dick to be his running mate,

00:08:09.879 --> 00:08:12.620
hoping that Nixon’s California roots would
win him the West.

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:15.259
It was Nixon’s ticket to the big leagues.

00:08:15.259 --> 00:08:17.169
It was also nearly his undoing.

00:08:17.169 --> 00:08:21.039
That September, the New York Post splashed
a story of a secret Nixon slush fund using

00:08:21.039 --> 00:08:23.810
campaign donations to support his lifestyle.

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:27.759
Although untrue, it nearly torpedoed Nixon’s
chances.

00:08:27.759 --> 00:08:29.500
Eisenhower called him and basically said:

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:30.500
“Look, Dick.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:34.030
Sort out this massive balls-up or I’ll find
a new VP.

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:35.030
Got it?”

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:36.800
Nixon got it.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.740
On September 23, 1952, Richard Nixon went
on TV to explain himself.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:46.690
It was the first-ever political speech televised
live, drawing a staggering 60 million viewers.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:51.740
Before them all, Nixon laid bare his finances
in painful, pitiful detail, painting a picture

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:54.040
of a struggling, relatable man.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:57.860
The speech was full of pathetic, human touches,
such as when Nixon confessed:

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:00.790
“Pat doesn't have a mink coat.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:06.790
But she does have a respectable Republican
cloth coat, and I always tell her she would

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:08.070
look good in anything.”

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:12.220
But what made the speech famous was when Nixon
announced he had been given one political

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:16.080
gift: a cocker spaniel named Checkers that
his children loved.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:18.960
And, damn it, they were gonna keep that dog.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.760
Known as the Checkers Speech today, the live
broadcast saved Nixon’s career.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:26.570
Eisenhower called him up and declared “you’re
my boy.”

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:30.730
That November 4, the Eisenhower-Nixon ticket
won in a landslide.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:34.640
It now seemed that Dick Nixon’s rise was
unstoppable.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:39.530
Rise, and Fall… and Rise

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:44.850
There’s an Eisenhower quote that brilliantly
sums up Nixon’s time as VP.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:49.210
Asked by a reporter what policies his Veep
had contributed, Eisenhower wryly replied:

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.420
“If you give me a week, I might think of
one.”

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:55.460
But while Nixon wasn’t transforming the
nation as VP, he was still working hard on

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:56.610
his main project:

00:09:56.610 --> 00:10:00.440
To make Richard Nixon look as much like a
future president as possible.

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:04.650
That meant doing stuff like going on a tour
of Venezuela, where he won praise for cooly

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:06.450
dealing with an anti-American mob.

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:10.340
It meant stuff like traveling to the USSR,
where he engaged Premier Nikita Khrushschev

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:13.070
in a spirited debate inside a model American
kitchen.

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:16.530
In this quest, Nixon was helped by Eisenhower’s
failing health.

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:21.280
Starting in 1955, Eisenhower suffered a series
of heart attacks that often left Nixon as

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:22.390
de-facto president.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:27.060
Despite worries he’d use his temporary role
as a bully pulpit, Nixon won plaudits as a

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:28.450
sober, sensible leader.

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:32.830
So, when Eisenhower’s second term expired
and it became time to pick the next Republican

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:35.540
nominee, everyone was squarely behind Nixon.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:40.510
With his experience, Nixon hoped the 1960
election would be a cakewalk, continuing his

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:41.720
unstoppable rise.

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:46.040
Unfortunately, the blue collar boy was about
to go slamming into the glass ceiling.

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:48.530
You all know the tale of the 1960 election.

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:53.150
How fresh-faced, vibrant John Kennedy completely
blew stuffy old Nixon out the water.

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:57.330
But while most people credit JFK’s media
nous and wise pick of Lyndon Johnson as his

00:10:57.330 --> 00:11:00.320
running mate for his victory, Nixon saw it
another way.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:04.270
As the presidency slipped from his grasp,
Nixon became convinced that the true culprit

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:07.770
wasn’t Kennedy’s charisma, or even the
recent recession.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:08.950
It was Kennedy’s money.

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:13.220
As far as Nixon was concerned, this was just
another case of the elites screwing over the

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:14.760
working class boy.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:19.290
That November, he felt he didn’t lose the
presidency so much as have it stolen from

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:20.290
him.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:23.840
It was the end of Nixon’s rise… and the
start of his long fall.

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:26.480
His career in tatters, Nixon returned to California.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:29.190
There, local Republicans convinced him to
run for governor.

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:33.590
Just two years after failing to become president,
Nixon lost California’s gubernatorial race

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:35.440
by a depressing margin.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.570
In the wake of the defeat, Nixon called a
press conference, where he bitterly declared:

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:44.370
“You won't have Nixon to kick around anymore,
because, gentlemen, this is my last press

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:45.370
conference."

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.690
What followed was a period in the political
wilderness.

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:51.860
Being the political wilderness, it was way
more comfortable than the actual wilderness,

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:56.140
and saw Nixon become rich for the first time
from his law practice.

00:11:56.140 --> 00:12:00.530
It also saw the former political streetfighter
refashion himself as an elder statesman, making

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.500
measured observations on the Vietnam War.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:06.320
However, Nixon was still politically-minded
enough to campaign for Barry Goldwater at

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:07.920
the 1964 election.

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:12.050
Although Goldwater lost, Nixon’s support
made him a darling of the Republican right.

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:17.130
It was almost like Nixon was courting conservatives,
building himself a powerbase in case he ever

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:19.180
decided to run for president again.

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:20.740
It was a shrewd move.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:25.020
As 1968 approached, the Republican party began
searching for its next standard-bearer.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:30.270
The obvious choice was an electrifying rising
star from California; a former actor christened

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:31.620
the future of the party.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:36.640
That actor’s name was Ronald Reagan, and
he’d go on to transform the GOP’s fortunes.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:38.590
But not in 1968.

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:42.570
As Reagan’s popularity grew, Nixon crept
back out the shadows.

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:47.120
Began approaching prominent conservatives,
ensuring they lined up behind him.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:50.490
By the time Reagan’s campaign really got
underway, the nomination had already been

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:51.490
sewn up.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:55.430
That August, 1968, Nixon was unanimously declared
Republican nominee.

00:12:55.430 --> 00:12:57.510
By now, the Democrats were in chaos.

00:12:57.510 --> 00:13:01.450
Lyndon B. Johnson had refused to run; the
convention had seen riots; and frontrunner

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:03.650
Bobby Kennedy had been assassinated.

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:06.750
Against that kinda backdrop, what else could
possibly happen?

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:11.780
On November 5, 1968, Richard Nixon swept the
electoral college to win the presidency.

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:15.960
His popular vote share - 43% - was one of
the lowest in history, eaten away by third

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:17.580
party candidate George Wallace.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:20.000
But a win was still a win.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:21.840
Against all the odds, he’d shown them.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:26.080
Shown those stuck up elites, those Kennedys,
those New York liberals.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:27.970
The Nixon era had now begun…

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:31.570
...and Tricky Dick was certain it would go
down in history.

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:34.030
Do You Remember…

00:13:34.030 --> 00:13:36.480
Your President Nixon?

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:41.920
Normally in a presidential Biographics, this
would be the point to talk you through their

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:44.810
term in office, hopefully with some jokes
thrown in.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:50.050
But, since this video is only 26 minutes,
and we’ve still got a massive thing called

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:55.860
Watergate to discuss, we’re gonna have to
just give you the barest overview of the Nixon

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:57.140
presidency.

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:00.500
And that’s a shame, because there’s so
much to talk about.

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:01.750
Take school busing.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:06.680
Although education had been desegregated under
Eisenhower, geographic separation meant most

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.850
schools were still mono-colored.

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:13.520
One solution was school busing, which involved
busing Black children into white schools.

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:17.140
Although Nixon was against the practice, a
Supreme Court ruling forced him to enforce

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:18.140
it.

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:19.140
Dick’s solution?

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:20.200
New Federalism.

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:24.900
At its heart, New Federalism was desegregation
- but done by local communities, rather than

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:25.900
the government.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:30.520
Biracial committees were established across
the South, each charged with implementing

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:31.520
desegregation.

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:34.390
Rather than simply passing the buck, it actually
worked.

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:39.190
Between Nixon’s election and 1970, the number
of Black kids in racially-segregated schools

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:42.730
dropped from 70 percent to only 18 percent.

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:43.980
Then there’s the environment.

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:48.270
If you’re below a certain age, the words
“Republican president” plus “environment”

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:53.530
probably conjure images of guys in expensive
suits gleefully pouring gallons of oil onto

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:55.040
baby seals.

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:56.220
But not Nixon.

00:14:56.220 --> 00:15:00.560
Tricky Dick was well aware that green issues
were a vote winner among moderate Democrats,

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:01.800
so he leaned into them.

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:06.120
It was on Nixon’s watch that the Environmental
Protection Agency was created, and the Clean

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:07.540
Air Act passed.

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:10.340
Another thing Dick Nixon supported was women’s
rights.

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:13.820
While Nixon’s environmentalism came mostly
from cynical vote-grabbing, his desire for

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:16.480
gender equality was mostly sincere.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:20.670
Nixon directed the Federal government to launch
affirmative action on women’s rights, and

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:25.330
passed legislation like Title IX, forbidding
sex discrimination in education.

00:15:25.330 --> 00:15:26.990
Then there was war.

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:32.940
It was Nixon who started both the War on Cancer,
the War on Drugs, plus the actual War in Cambodia

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.200
after he authorized US incursions.

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:39.690
That last one, by the way, led to one of the
most infamous non-Watergate moments of his

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:40.690
presidency.

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:44.420
On May 4, 1970, peaceful demonstrators gathered
at Kent State.

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:49.620
There, before hundreds of witnesses, the Ohio
National Guard shot dead four unarmed people,

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:50.970
and injured another nine.

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:55.380
Despite massive public outcry, the Nixon White
House didn’t prosecute any guardsmen.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:58.780
The last major Nixon initiative we should
talk about is China.

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:02.690
In February, 1972, Nixon surprised the world
by going to visit the Middle Kingdom.

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:06.630
“Surprised” because, until that point,
China had been America’s boogeyman number

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:08.390
2, right behind the USSR.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:13.330
But Nixon realized he could keep the USSR
in check by cozying up to Beijing.

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:17.380
Although the move was cynical, it’s now
seen as the moment Communist China started

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:19.050
opening up to the world.

00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:20.050
So there you have it.

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:24.610
Four years of Nixon - four of the most dramatic
years in modern US history - condensed into

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:26.070
about 400 words.

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:30.620
And we still had to leave so much out; from
the Oil Crisis, to ending the draft, to the

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:35.390
Nixon Shock that ended Bretton-Woods and set
the stage for a decade of stagflation.

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:40.560
Still, at least you’re now aware there was
more to the Nixon presidency than merely scandal,

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:44.480
especially as we move towards the biggest
scandal of all.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:46.530
It’s finally time to talk about Watergate.

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:51.490
Watergate - Part One

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:57.980
The Beastie Boys once sang “I’m-a set
it straight, this Watergate,” and that’s

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:00.620
exactly what I’m gonna do for you right
now.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:03.590
Wish me luck, because it’s super complicated.

00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:07.659
Although Nixon’s first term was outwardly
successful, behind the scenes, Tricky Dick’s

00:17:07.659 --> 00:17:08.659
paranoia was increasing.

00:17:08.659 --> 00:17:14.209
In February of 1971, the 37th President installed
a secret taping system in the White House,

00:17:14.209 --> 00:17:16.789
one activated the moment anyone entered the
room.

00:17:16.789 --> 00:17:19.890
But the real crazy started on June 13, 1971.

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:24.149
That was the day the New York Times published
the Pentagon Papers - top secret documents

00:17:24.149 --> 00:17:27.829
laying bare American atrocities and incompetence
in Vietnam.

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:32.240
While the papers stopped with Lyndon Johnson’s
White House, Nixon was still outraged at the

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:33.240
leak.

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:35.809
It was from this outrage that the White House
Plumbers were formed.

00:17:35.809 --> 00:17:40.210
A top-secret team, the Plumbers were there
to stop leaks through the use of spying, sabotage,

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:41.210
and break-ins.

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:44.610
Their first act was to break into the office
of the shrink who treated the Pentagon Papers’

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:45.610
leaker.

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:47.960
It wouldn’t be the last time the Plumbers
got involved in something shady.

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:50.730
OK, let’s fast forward a year now, to 1972.

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:55.179
Nixon is up for reelection, and his most-likely
opponent is the formidable Democratic Senator,

00:17:55.179 --> 00:17:56.179
Edmund Muskie.

00:17:56.179 --> 00:17:59.559
That spring, a bizarre string of problems
beset Muskie.

00:17:59.559 --> 00:18:03.840
White voters in early states were harassed
by late night phone calls from rude people

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:06.620
who claimed they were Black supporters of
Muskie.

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:10.559
Others received letters on Muskie stationary
that spewed absurd conspiracy theories about

00:18:10.559 --> 00:18:12.240
his primary opponents.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:15.580
Yep, it was Nixon, back to his old negative
campaigning.

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:20.159
But now he had help from both the Plumbers,
and the Campaign to Reelect the President

00:18:20.159 --> 00:18:22.950
- pleasingly abbreviated to CREEP.

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:26.409
And CREEP would stop at nothing to fulfil
their mission.

00:18:26.409 --> 00:18:31.210
By April, the pressure had torpedoed Muskie’s
run, but still the Plumbers were at it.

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:36.860
At 2a.m. on June 17, 1972, they broke into
Democrat Party HQ in the Watergate Office

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:38.700
Building, intending to bug the place.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:41.090
Instead, they got caught by a security guard.

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:45.350
Within two days, the Washington Post was reporting
that one of the burglars was a salaried member

00:18:45.350 --> 00:18:48.649
of CREEP, but Nixon assumed it would blow
over.

00:18:48.649 --> 00:18:51.820
He’s actually on tape declaring the story
will die, and saying

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:54.559
“Who the hell's going to keep it alive?”

00:18:54.559 --> 00:18:55.740
The two-name answer?

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:57.960
Bob Woodward and Carl Bernstein.

00:18:57.960 --> 00:19:02.110
All through that hot summer, the Post reporters
published articles covering CREEP’s dirty

00:19:02.110 --> 00:19:07.250
tricks, and linking a Nixon check for $28,000
to one of the Watergate burglars.

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:09.659
But no-one was paying any attention.

00:19:09.659 --> 00:19:13.730
Behind the scenes, Nixon was ordering the
CIA to interfere with the FBI’s Watergate

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:14.730
investigation.

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:18.909
Out in the open, the Democrats had nominated
leftwing populist George McGovern as their

00:19:18.909 --> 00:19:21.509
candidate, and Nixon was making mincemeat
out of him.

00:19:21.509 --> 00:19:26.419
Despite the steady trickle of Watergate stories,
Nixon crushed McGovern in ‘72, winning one

00:19:26.419 --> 00:19:28.669
of the biggest landslides in US history.

00:19:28.669 --> 00:19:32.960
Seriously, the only comparable victories are
FDR in ‘36 and Reagan in ‘84.

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:37.270
But while the voters had agreed to re-insert
Dick into the White House for a second term,

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:40.039
the wheels were already coming off the presidency.

00:19:40.039 --> 00:19:44.649
Just days before Nixon’s inauguration, the
seven men involved in the Watergate break-in

00:19:44.649 --> 00:19:45.800
went on trial.

00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:48.549
Perhaps Nixon expected them to stay loyal
to him.

00:19:48.549 --> 00:19:50.649
If so, his trust was misplaced.

00:19:50.649 --> 00:19:55.769
Over the next few months, these seven men
would nearly all choose saving their own skins

00:19:55.769 --> 00:19:57.000
over saving Nixon.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:04.340
Tricky Dick couldn’t see it yet, but his
presidency was already doomed.

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:07.350
The Fall

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:11.620
Richard Nixon’s second term is a little
like reading Shakespearean tragedy reimagined

00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:13.610
as depressing farce.

00:20:13.610 --> 00:20:17.009
Barely was Nixon back in the Oval Office than
the walls started closing in.

00:20:17.009 --> 00:20:21.830
On February 7, the Democrat-controlled Senate
passed Resolution 60, establishing the Watergate

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:22.830
Hearings.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:24.659
Initially, this didn’t bother Nixon much.

00:20:24.659 --> 00:20:29.809
He went on TV to assure viewers he was blameless,
that the fault lay with some rouge aides.

00:20:29.809 --> 00:20:31.169
Most people believed him.

00:20:31.169 --> 00:20:34.409
But then May rolled around and things started
to unravel.

00:20:34.409 --> 00:20:37.809
On the 18th, special prosecutor Archibald
Cox was appointed.

00:20:37.809 --> 00:20:41.159
Shortly thereafter, the secret White House
taping system was discovered.

00:20:41.159 --> 00:20:43.590
Not unreasonably, Cox was all like:

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:46.519
“Oh, so you’ve been recording your conversations
this entire time?

00:20:46.519 --> 00:20:48.549
Mind if I have a listen?”

00:20:48.549 --> 00:20:50.539
To which Nixon was all like:

00:20:50.539 --> 00:20:51.539
“Err.

00:20:51.539 --> 00:20:52.539
No.

00:20:52.539 --> 00:20:55.629
But I will tell you what’s on the tapes:
they definitely, definitely prove I’m innocent!”

00:20:55.629 --> 00:20:58.929
As the clamour grew to release the tapes,
Nixon’s paranoia reached new heights.

00:20:58.929 --> 00:21:03.690
In his head, it was everything he’d ever
feared: shadowy elites teaming up with a Jewish

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:08.170
reporter to screw over poor, blue collar Dick
Nixon.

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:12.210
Convinced he had enemies everywhere, the 37th
President lashed out in the most self-destructive

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:13.320
way possible.

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:18.399
The Saturday Night Massacre on October 20,
1973, was a turning point in the scandal.

00:21:18.399 --> 00:21:22.360
In quick succession, Nixon fired both his
Attorney General and Deputy Attorney General

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:26.649
for refusing to fire Archibald Cox, before
his Solicitor General agreed to carry out

00:21:26.649 --> 00:21:27.649
the order.

00:21:27.649 --> 00:21:31.429
Half an hour later, Nixon ordered the FBI
to seal off Cox’s offices.

00:21:31.429 --> 00:21:34.399
It was supposed to kill the investigation
through force.

00:21:34.399 --> 00:21:36.380
Instead, it created outrage.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:38.350
In the House, impeachment charges were filed.

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:41.250
Fellow Republicans began to speak out against
their president.

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:45.580
Chastised, Nixon was forced to hire a new
special prosecutor, Leon Jaworski.

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:49.019
By now, it was clear the president had something
to hide.

00:21:49.019 --> 00:21:51.879
The rest of the story plays out with painful
inevitability.

00:21:51.879 --> 00:21:55.999
There’s the infamous “I’m not a crook
speech” in November, 1973.

00:21:55.999 --> 00:21:59.850
The March, 1974 indictment of seven Nixon
aides by a Grand Jury.

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:01.850
There’s the subpoena for the White House
tapes.

00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:06.779
The Supreme Court battle that ruled against
Nixon on July 24, forcing him to release them.

00:22:06.779 --> 00:22:08.529
After that, it was all over.

00:22:08.529 --> 00:22:12.320
On August 5, 1974, the first transcripts of
the tapes appeared in the media.

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:16.600
They included Nixon directing the CIA to interfere
with the FBI’s investigation.

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:19.129
The press dubbed this recording “the Smoking
Gun”.

00:22:19.129 --> 00:22:23.820
The strangest thing about Watergate in hindsight
is that Nixon really didn’t have anything

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:25.100
to do with the break-in.

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:29.700
CREEP and the Plumbers really had gone rouge,
and Nixon could’ve probably weathered that

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:30.940
smaller scandal.

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:32.639
Instead, he chose to cover it up.

00:22:32.639 --> 00:22:34.610
And that was a much bigger deal.

00:22:34.610 --> 00:22:38.309
Following the release of the transcripts,
Congress moved to impeach Nixon.

00:22:38.309 --> 00:22:41.730
It’s almost certain the votes were there
to remove him from office.

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:44.110
But Nixon never gave them the chance.

00:22:44.110 --> 00:22:49.529
On August 8, 1974, Richard Nixon announced
his resignation - the only president in history

00:22:49.529 --> 00:22:50.610
to do so.

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:53.519
At noon the next day, he and his family left
by helicopter.

00:22:53.519 --> 00:22:58.220
As they departed, Nixon turned, grinned, and
flashed a V sign for “victory”.

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:00.399
And so ended Dick Nixon’s presidency.

00:23:00.399 --> 00:23:04.360
Less than a month later, on September 8, new
president Gerald Ford issued a blanket pardon

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:07.110
for any crimes Richard Nixon may have committed
while in office.

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:11.529
It was a hugely unpopular move, and one that
likely handed the Democrats the White House

00:23:11.529 --> 00:23:14.470
in 1976, but there was nothing anyone could
do.

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:18.110
Dick Nixon was now, and would always be, a
free man.

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:22.460
The only question now was what to do with
all this unexpected freedom.

00:23:22.460 --> 00:23:26.679
The End of All That

00:23:26.679 --> 00:23:31.170
That’s not a huge amount to say about Nixon’s
life after the White House.

00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:34.400
He and Pat moved back to California, settled
in San Clemente.

00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:38.799
At first, the disgraced president kept his
head low, but legal fights had so sapped his

00:23:38.799 --> 00:23:41.910
bank account that he returned to public life
just to get his money back.

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:46.539
And so we got the unedifying spectacle of
the Frost-Nixon interviews, which earned Nixon

00:23:46.539 --> 00:23:51.110
$600,000; and the publication of his memoirs,
which netted him $2m.

00:23:51.110 --> 00:23:55.769
Yet even though Nixon remained a hugely divisive
figure with the public, those on Capitol Hill

00:23:55.769 --> 00:23:57.250
could never quite let go of him.

00:23:57.250 --> 00:24:00.799
Across the next fifteen years, Presidents
Jimmy Carter, Ronald Reagan, and George H.W.

00:24:00.799 --> 00:24:05.720
Bush all came to consult with Tricky Dick,
searching out his expertise in foreign policy.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:10.070
By 1990, the image of Nixon as an elder statesman
had so entered the political class that three

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:12.799
ex-Presidents came to the opening of his library.

00:24:12.799 --> 00:24:15.909
In a weird way, this was everything Nixon
had ever dreamed of.

00:24:15.909 --> 00:24:19.720
Here he was, a man who had committed high
crimes and misdemeanours, yet had not only

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:22.370
never faced justice; but was now being publicly
honored.

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:27.009
Does that sound like a blue collar kid from
the sticks… or like a member of the privileged

00:24:27.009 --> 00:24:28.009
elite?

00:24:28.009 --> 00:24:30.830
Richard Nixon finally died on April 22, 1994.

00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:35.399
At his funeral, President Bill Clinton gave
a stirring eulogy that ended with the words:

00:24:35.399 --> 00:24:39.679
“May the day of judging President Nixon
on anything less than his entire life and

00:24:39.679 --> 00:24:41.809
career come to a close.”

00:24:41.809 --> 00:24:46.360
The political rehabilitation of Richard Nixon
was now so total that Watergate was seemingly

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:47.360
forgotten.

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:49.409
But the story doesn’t end there.

00:24:49.409 --> 00:24:53.490
Today, the Watergate scandal is older than…
well, me for starters; plus major stuff like

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:59.260
Star Wars, the Iranian Revolution, and entire
countries like Angola, Slovakia, or Mozambique.

00:24:59.260 --> 00:25:01.409
In short, it’s history.

00:25:01.409 --> 00:25:03.830
Something that happened in the dim and distant
past.

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:05.990
Yet, somehow, we still remember it.

00:25:05.990 --> 00:25:10.820
In a world where even major political scandals
like Iran-Contra or the Pentagon Papers have

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:14.429
slipped from the public’s mind, that’s
saying something.

00:25:14.429 --> 00:25:16.869
For whatever reason, Watergate has become
only more-notorious.

00:25:16.869 --> 00:25:20.370
It’s now the gold standard of scandal.

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:24.769
No matter how hard the political class may
have tried, it will always stain Nixon’s

00:25:24.769 --> 00:25:25.769
name,

00:25:25.769 --> 00:25:30.429
And yet, there’s no denying there was more
to the 37th President than a single scandal.

00:25:30.429 --> 00:25:33.429
Richard Nixon a complex, flawed, and brilliant
man.

00:25:33.429 --> 00:25:37.890
He was insecure and bullying; but also a man
who understood the pain of being an outsider.

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:41.710
An ordinary man who really thought he could
change his country for the better.

00:25:41.710 --> 00:25:45.100
Too often in history and politics, we like
to paint simple narratives.

00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:49.150
This man is a monster, this man is a saint,
blah, blah, blah…

00:25:49.150 --> 00:25:54.610
But real life is never so simple, and men
like Nixon are never simply caricatures, cartoon

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:56.379
villains there to be laughed at.

00:25:56.379 --> 00:26:01.440
They’re human beings who really hurt, really
care… and sometimes even manage to do some

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:02.440
good.

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:06.260
Richard Nixon may be long gone now, but the
lessons we learned from his presidency should

00:26:06.260 --> 00:26:07.309
stay with us forever.

00:26:07.309 --> 00:26:09.360
It’s easy to hate such a man.

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:32.330
It takes real guts to try to understand him.

